WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.155
Going durable gave the tree durable grain
storage and a durable write-ahead log.

2
00:00:06.155 --> 00:00:09.336
Now we can bring an existing dataset
into it.

3
00:00:09.686 --> 00:00:14.823
A move from Redis, a relational database
or Cosmos DB is a one-way import.

4
00:00:14.823 --> 00:00:19.751
Bulk load seeds a tree; it does
not keep the source and target in sync.

5
00:00:20.751 --> 00:00:25.833
Before the first entry, settle three
things: the ingest path, the key you

6
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:31.540
compose from each source record, and the
encoding that turns its value into bytes.

7
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:36.622
Changing keys later is another migration;
values stay opaque to the core.

8
00:00:37.622 --> 00:00:39.575
If the dataset fits in memory,

9
00:00:39.575 --> 00:00:42.896
the list-based BulkLoadAsync sorts entries
for you.

10
00:00:42.896 --> 00:00:43.938
For larger sets,

11
00:00:43.938 --> 00:00:48.886
stream them in strictly ascending ordinal
key order from inside the cluster.

12
00:00:48.886 --> 00:00:50.449
It does not check order:

13
00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:54.616
out-of-order input is accepted
and leaves the tree out of order.

14
00:00:54.616 --> 00:00:55.397
Redis scans,

15
00:00:55.397 --> 00:01:00.020
database collations and Cosmos ordering
may not match the tree's order.

16
00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:05.103
If the loader runs outside the cluster,
use the tree-administration session.

17
00:01:05.103 --> 00:01:09.587
It acknowledges chunks and lets you
resume the last unacknowledged chunk

18
00:01:09.587 --> 00:01:11.891
with the same operation id and index.

19
00:01:11.891 --> 00:01:15.192
It checks order within each chunk,
not across chunks,

20
00:01:15.192 --> 00:01:17.372
so keep the whole stream ascending.

21
00:01:17.372 --> 00:01:18.929
When the source is paged,

22
00:01:18.929 --> 00:01:22.977
persist its cursor with the chunk index
and resume both together.

23
00:01:23.977 --> 00:01:26.088
Design keys for the tree's order:

24
00:01:26.088 --> 00:01:30.503
compare ordinally, and zero-pad
numeric segments so ten follows nine.

25
00:01:30.503 --> 00:01:33.126
Choose the value encoding before loading.

26
00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:37.157
Bulk load does not enforce configured key
or value size bounds,

27
00:01:37.157 --> 00:01:39.076
so check them in the producer.

28
00:01:39.426 --> 00:01:42.853
Use the source store's own
rules to take a consistent cut,

29
00:01:42.853 --> 00:01:45.335
then load those entries into a fresh tree.

30
00:01:45.685 --> 00:01:50.048
Compare the loaded count with the cut
and spot-check range boundaries.

31
00:01:50.398 --> 00:01:55.374
Apply changes since that cut with
SetManyAsync; handle deletions separately.

32
00:01:55.374 --> 00:01:57.011
That delta is not atomic.

33
00:01:57.011 --> 00:02:00.874
Move readers only when the checks
and changes are complete,

34
00:02:00.874 --> 00:02:03.493
and keep the source readable until then.

35
00:02:04.493 --> 00:02:06.788
That is the end of the Build path.

36
00:02:06.788 --> 00:02:09.758
Next, the Samples page
in the documentation.

37
00:02:09.758 --> 00:02:13.673
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.
